WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.080
These two monkeyflowers can be crossed by hand, and the cross makes hybrid seed.

2
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.280
So why do almost no genes pass between them in the wild?

3
00:00:13.383 --> 00:00:17.943
Gene flow is the movement of genes between populations through mating.

4
00:00:19.343 --> 00:00:20.443
Picture it as a stream.

5
00:00:21.783 --> 00:00:25.463
Each gold dot is one chance for genes to cross to the other species.

6
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:31.927
A reproductive barrier is anything that reduces gene flow between populations.

7
00:00:33.347 --> 00:00:35.707
You might expect one wall that stops everything.

8
00:00:37.087 --> 00:00:39.027
These monkeyflowers have no such wall.

9
00:00:40.347 --> 00:00:41.727
Each of their barriers is partial.

10
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:46.507
So picture the barriers as gates, in the order life meets them.

11
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:49.107
First, where the plants grow.

12
00:00:50.347 --> 00:00:51.987
Then, which pollinator visits.

13
00:00:53.327 --> 00:00:56.627
Then, whether the other species' pollen can fertilize the egg.

14
00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.990
Fertilization makes the zygote, the fertilized egg.

15
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:03.810
Gates before it are prezygotic.

16
00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:09.710
Gates after it, such as hybrids that fail to survive or are sterile, are

17
00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:10.710
postzygotic.

18
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:17.833
Here is the key. When gates act one after another, each acts only on what earlier

19
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:18.833
gates let through.

20
00:01:19.973 --> 00:01:24.713
A pollinator cannot carry pollen between plants that never grow near each other.

21
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:30.867
Try three partial gates, each stopping seventy percent of the dots that reach it.

22
00:01:32.307 --> 00:01:34.367
Together, do they stop seventy percent?

23
00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:36.107
More than a hundred?

24
00:01:37.527 --> 00:01:38.527
Something else?

25
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.000
Watch the counter.

26
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.260
Each gate leaves less for the next one.

27
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.160
In the end, about ninety-seven dots in every hundred are stopped.

28
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.580
That stops more than any single gate, yet a few dots still pass all three.

29
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.080
That is the rule. The fractions that get through multiply.

30
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.000
Several leaky gates in a row can block nearly all gene flow.

31
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.360
Adding their strengths is wrong, because the sum can pass one hundred percent.

32
00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:19.043
Now the real monkeyflowers.

33
00:02:19.043 --> 00:02:23.673
Ramsey, Bradshaw and Schemske measured these gates one stage at a time.

34
00:02:24.813 --> 00:02:26.913
They worked in the field and in the greenhouse.

35
00:02:28.313 --> 00:02:29.413
Every gate was partial.

36
00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:34.783
Where the plants grow, and which pollinator visits, came first in the sequence.

37
00:02:36.003 --> 00:02:39.363
Most of the stream was gone before any hybrid could form.

38
00:02:40.417 --> 00:02:45.857
Together, the measured barriers were estimated to block, on average, almost all gene

39
00:02:45.857 --> 00:02:49.337
flow. No one gate could explain that alone.

40
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.260
This is also why a species pair does not need sterile hybrids.

41
00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.640
When the early gates are strong, few hybrids form at all.

42
00:02:59.683 --> 00:03:02.003
So two species need no perfect wall.

43
00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:08.423
Several leaky gates, acting one after another, can cut the gene flow between them to

44
00:03:08.423 --> 00:03:08.763
a trickle.
